WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.128
High impact starts with confidentiality, integrity, and availability.

00:00:03.128 --> 00:00:05.398
Confidentiality is about preventing unauthorized disclosure.

00:00:05.398 --> 00:00:10.697
Integrity is about preventing improper modification or loss of trust in information and processing.

00:00:10.697 --> 00:00:15.616
Availability is about making sure the service and data remain usable when needed.

00:00:15.616 --> 00:00:20.158
A high-impact context exists when a failure in one or more of

00:00:20.158 --> 00:00:23.564
these areas could cause severe or catastrophic adverse effects.

00:00:23.564 --> 00:00:26.970
High-impact cloud services may handle sensitive unclassified federal data,

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:30.376
support mission-critical operations, affect public trust, influence privacy outcomes,

00:00:30.376 --> 00:00:35.296
or support activities where disruption can cascade into major operational or financial harm.

00:00:35.296 --> 00:00:36.431
In some contexts,

00:00:36.431 --> 00:00:41.351
protection-of-life considerations or essential public services may be part of the risk picture.

00:00:41.351 --> 00:00:45.135
The baseline should reflect the federal mission and data risk,

00:00:45.135 --> 00:00:47.406
not the provider's preferred sales position.

00:00:47.406 --> 00:00:48.541
Service dependency matters.

00:00:48.541 --> 00:00:53.083
A system that looks ordinary in isolation can become high impact if

00:00:53.083 --> 00:00:55.354
many agency workflows depend on it,

00:00:55.354 --> 00:00:57.246
if outages have wide consequences,

00:00:57.246 --> 00:01:01.787
or if the service acts as a critical platform for other systems.

00:01:01.787 --> 00:01:06.707
Data sensitivity also matters, but availability and integrity can be just as important.

00:01:06.707 --> 00:01:12.384
A high-impact decision should consider the whole mission context rather than a single data label.

00:01:12.384 --> 00:01:17.304
The engineering implication is that High cannot be treated as a documentation exercise.

00:01:17.304 --> 00:01:21.088
Severe consequences require stronger attention to architecture, access control, monitoring,

00:01:21.088 --> 00:01:23.737
response, recovery, dependency management, and operational maturity.

00:01:23.737 --> 00:01:27.900
Teams should be able to explain not only what controls exist,

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:32.820
but why those controls are suitable for the mission risk the service supports.
